#let terms = (
  (slug: "term-decision-point", title: [Decision point], body: [A declared place in a system where a recommender proposes an output of one type and that output reaches a gated tool only through a policy or a verdict. Its declaration is a `Point`: a stable name, an output codec, the verdicts it allows, its declared facts, and its timing. _Avoid_: gate — the gate is the gated tool's guard, not the place; checkpoint.]),
  (slug: "term-occurrence", title: [Occurrence], body: [One instance of a decision point: one recommendation about one subject and everything that happened to it afterwards. Its identity is an id that is either the admission key or a new id from the port. _Avoid_: case, ticket — a ticket is something else here.]),
  (slug: "term-entry", title: [Entry], body: [One appended fact in an occurrence's record: a sequence number, the store-clock time it was appended at, and an event of one of seventeen kinds. An entry is never changed or removed.]),
  (slug: "term-record", title: [Record], body: [The ordered list of an occurrence's entries. It is the only authority on where the occurrence stands and the only data the system learns from. _Avoid_: log, history — both suggest something beside the truth rather than the truth.]),
  (slug: "term-status", title: [Status], body: [Where an occurrence stands, computed by folding its record: recommended, awaiting, accepted, held, released, rejected, expired, withdrawn, or refused. A status is never stored as authority; a head carries a copy of it.]),
  (slug: "term-settled", title: [Settled occurrence], body: [An occurrence whose status is released, rejected, expired, withdrawn, or refused. Its outcome can no longer change; only reviews, assessments, effect reports, recorded refusals, and the showings that lead to a review are appended after it settles.]),
  (slug: "term-desk", title: [Desk], body: [A decision point bound to the gated tool it drives, the function that reads its current policy, a storage port, and an optional consult handler. A desk is a plain value that holds no state; its verbs run in the caller's transaction. _Avoid_: engine, service — no process stands behind it.]),
  (slug: "term-verb", title: [Verb], body: [One operation of a desk on the record, such as `recommend`, `decide` or `wake`. Every verb loads and locks the occurrence, reads the store clock, checks, and appends; a verb that returns an error has written nothing the caller may keep.]),
  (slug: "term-refusal", title: [Refusal], body: [The typed reason a verb did not do what was asked, a value of `decisions.Refusal`. A refusal is a value the caller branches on, never text to parse; its transaction is rolled back.]),
  (slug: "term-disposition", title: [Disposition], body: [The class of a refusal that says what a deliverer may do after it: `Transient` (contention or an unknown outcome, deliver again uncounted), `Retry` (may never pass, counted), `Poisoned` (needs an operator, set aside), or `Final` (the occurrence moved on, the job is done).]),
  (slug: "term-proposal", title: [Proposal], body: [What a recommender hands to `recommend`: the trigger, the subject, the context, the recommendation, and optionally the person who asked and what the occurrence hangs from. It is opaque and built only through `decisions.proposal`.]),
  (slug: "term-trigger", title: [Trigger], body: [What asked for a decision, such as a message, a run, or a build, named by a kind and an id. A trigger is never the model.]),
  (slug: "term-subject", title: [Subject], body: [Who or what a gated tool call is about, such as a person, a company, or a service, named by a kind and an id. A guard reads the subject and superseding finds occurrences by it.]),
  (slug: "term-requester", title: [Requester], body: [The person who asked for an occurrence: the proposal's `asked_by`, or the person whose own act the recommendation is. Eligibility that says "someone else" excludes the requester.]),
  (slug: "term-parent", title: [Parent], body: [What an occurrence hangs from so that settling it can start what follows: a run, or the occurrence that consulted it.]),
  (slug: "term-bundle", title: [Bundle], body: [The occurrences one recommendation proposed together, possibly at several points, named by a key and holding a known number of members, built with `vocabulary.bundle`. A member's key is the bundle's key, a slash and its item, a name without a slash built with `vocabulary.item`. Each member is an ordinary occurrence; a bundle is complete when it holds every member and each has settled. _Avoid_: batch, group, run — each already names something else.]),
  (slug: "term-recommender", title: [Recommender], body: [What recommended an output, as a versioned value: rules, a classifier, one model call, an agent, or a person acting on their own authority. Agreement and accuracy are counted per recommender value, so a new version is a new value.]),
  (slug: "term-recommendation", title: [Recommendation], body: [A recommender's proposed output with its recommender, its explanation, its signals, and the reads the model received. Every kind of recommender produces the same recommendation and differs only in its explanation.]),
  (slug: "term-own-act", title: [Own act], body: [A recommendation whose recommender is a person, standing for that person's own action. The policy condition `OwnAct` matches it, and its person is its requester.]),
  (slug: "term-context", title: [Context], body: [The input a model started from: a list of references to immutable data and the version of the renderer that turns them into a prompt, a card, and an example. In code it is a `Manifest`. _Avoid_: prompt — the prompt is rendered from the context.]),
  (slug: "term-reference", title: [Reference], body: [A pointer to immutable data: a kind, an id, and the revision that names the exact value read. For a read, the kind is the read's name and the id its argument.]),
  (slug: "term-read", title: [Read], body: [A named lookup a model makes beyond its context, with one argument, answered with content and the reference to the evidence that holds it. The record keeps the reads the model received, never the reads it merely called.]),
  (slug: "term-evidence", title: [Evidence], body: [Content captured from an outside system before anyone sees it, stored under a reference whose revision is derived from the content. Evidence is never changed.]),
  (slug: "term-observed-reads", title: [Observed reads], body: [The counting mode in which the reads a model received are the reads its sources answered while it ran. It is the default, for a model that uses every answer it asks for.]),
  (slug: "term-reported-reads", title: [Reported reads], body: [The counting mode in which a model names the reads it received in its recommendation and the library believes that report. It is for an agent loop whose tool can read and then fail.]),
  (slug: "term-model", title: [Model], body: [A function from a context and the sources it may read to a recommendation or a typed failure, called only through `model.run`. The same model value runs in production and under replay.]),
  (slug: "term-sources", title: [Sources], body: [Everything a model may read: a function that resolves a context reference and a function that answers a named read. In production they capture; under replay the record answers and nothing else does.]),
  (slug: "term-model-failure", title: [Model failure], body: [The typed reason a model has no recommendation: not recorded, read failed, deadline, raised, undecodable, or declined. Each variant says whether a retry can help.]),
  (slug: "term-failed-attempt", title: [Failed attempt], body: [An entry that records a model failure under an admission key before any recommendation exists, appended in a transaction of its own. A record may begin with failed attempts; its recommendation follows them.]),
  (slug: "term-fact", title: [Fact], body: [A named number, text, or flag that a policy may read. A fact comes from one source: the output, the recommender's signals, a consulted point's answer, or the time waited.]),
  (slug: "term-fact-key", title: [Fact key], body: [A typed name for a fact, built once with `fact.number`, `fact.text` or `fact.flag` and used by the declaration, by a policy written in Gleam, and by a model's signals.]),
  (slug: "term-declared-fact", title: [Declared fact], body: [A fact the decision point names, with its type and its one source: computed from the output (`decisions.fact`), a signal arriving beside it (`decisions.signal`), or a consulted point's answer (`decisions.answer`). Declaring a name again replaces its earlier declaration. A policy may read only declared facts and the time waited.]),
  (slug: "term-signal", title: [Signal], body: [A declared fact that the recommendation carries beside its output, such as a model's score. A signal never stands for an output fact or a consulted point's answer.]),
  (slug: "term-answer", title: [Answer], body: [A declared fact that one named consulted point returns through `inform`, such as a compliance check's verdict. Only that point can give it.]),
  (slug: "term-policy", title: [Policy], body: [One version of the data that holds every condition for proceeding without a person: a version number, ordered rules, and a default outcome. It reads only the recommender and declared facts.]),
  (slug: "term-policy-version", title: [Policy version], body: [The number that names one published policy. Every entry that records a policy answer records the version that gave it.]),
  (slug: "term-publishable", title: [Publishable policy], body: [A policy that reads only facts its point declares, each as its declared type. A policy is always well formed, since `policy.new` refuses one that asks for no approval or samples outside 0 to 100. The desk refuses to use a policy that is not publishable.]),
  (slug: "term-outcome", title: [Policy outcome], body: [What a policy says about a recommendation: proceed with a sample, ask whoever an eligibility admits, or consult another decision point.]),
  (slug: "term-timeline", title: [Timeline], body: [A policy's answer now and each later answer it will give as the occurrence goes on waiting, computed once when the policy is applied and kept on the record as steps.]),
  (slug: "term-step", title: [Step], body: [One later answer of a timeline: after a whole number of minutes waited, the policy says a given outcome.]),
  (slug: "term-waited", title: [Time waited], body: [Whole minutes since the recommendation was appended, by the store clock. It is the fact `waited_minutes`, which every policy may read and no point declares.]),
  (slug: "term-eligibility", title: [Eligibility], body: [Who may give the verdict an occurrence waits for: anyone holding a decision set, someone else holding it, whoever any of several eligibilities admits, or a number of different validators each eligible under another eligibility.]),
  (slug: "term-decision-set", title: [Decision set], body: [A named group of validators, such as `finance`. Who holds a set is a fact of the validator's session, never of the record.]),
  (slug: "term-validator", title: [Validator], body: [A person or another system that gives verdicts, with the decision sets it holds in the current session.]),
  (slug: "term-card", title: [Card], body: [An occurrence as it was shown to one validator, and the only value from which a verdict can be given. Only `open` and `resume` construct one.]),
  (slug: "term-showing", title: [Showing], body: [The entry that records that a card was shown to a validator, with the inputs that had changed since the model read them. A verdict names the showing it was given on.]),
  (slug: "term-ticket", title: [Ticket], body: [What a card is known by outside the process that holds it: the occurrence id and the sequence number of its showing. `resume` turns a ticket back into the same card.]),
  (slug: "term-verdict", title: [Verdict], body: [A validator's judgment of the output that stands: approve, reject with a reason, or correct with an output of the point's own type and a reason.]),
  (slug: "term-correction", title: [Correction], body: [A verdict that replaces the output that stands with another output of the point's type. It counts as its author's approval of the new output.]),
  (slug: "term-standing-output", title: [Standing output], body: [The output a verdict on a card would be about: the recommended output, or the output of the latest correction. Approvals count only for the output that stands.]),
  (slug: "term-reason", title: [Reason], body: [A machine-readable category and an optional note that a rejection or a correction must carry.]),
  (slug: "term-missing-context", title: [Missing context], body: [The reason category `missing_context`: the validator knows something the card does not show. A verdict with it requires a note by the same validator.]),
  (slug: "term-decider", title: [Decider], body: [Who accepted the final output: a policy version, or the validator whose verdict completed the acceptance.]),
  (slug: "term-rollback", title: [Rollback token], body: [The opaque value a refused release leaves inside its refusal: the point, the occurrence, the validator, the showing, the verdict, and the reason. `record_refusal` accepts nothing else.]),
  (slug: "term-guard", title: [Guard], body: [A function of the transaction and the subject that states a fact about the world, returning the evidence it relied on or a `Stop`: `Refuse` for this release, or `Unsure` when it could not tell. Its parameters carry no output.]),
  (slug: "term-gated-tool", title: [Gated tool], body: [A named guard and an executor that accepts only a released output. It is the one way an output reaches the world.]),
  (slug: "term-release", title: [Release], body: [The step in which an accepted output passes its guard and reaches its gated tool's executor, inside a savepoint, in the transaction that appends the `released` entry. An occurrence has at most one release.]),
  (slug: "term-released-output", title: [Released output], body: [The opaque value an executor receives for one release attempt inside the savepoint: the final output with its occurrence, release key, subject, context, parent and decider. Only the desk constructs one, and only for the executor.]),
  (slug: "term-release-key", title: [Release key], body: [The stable key of a release, `release:` followed by the occurrence id. It is the idempotency key sent to an outside system and the key by which an uncertain effect is looked up.]),
  (slug: "term-effect", title: [Effect], body: [What became of a release outside the database: performed with a receipt, not performed with a reason, or uncertain. The latest reported effect counts.]),
  (slug: "term-release-check", title: [Release check], body: [A function the desk is given with `checking` that receives a `ToRelease` value (output, subject, context, key) during a release, after the guard and before the executor, and returns the evidence it relied on or a `Stop`. No executor accepts its value.]),
  (slug: "term-policy-hold", title: [Policy hold], body: [A hold carried by a policy outcome, `Proceed(sample:, hold:)`, before releasing what the policy accepted by itself: an undo window on autonomous decisions, published as data with the policy version.]),
  (slug: "term-withdrawer", title: [Withdrawer], body: [A holder of the decision set a point names with `withdrawn_by`, who may withdraw a held occurrence besides its decider.]),
  (slug: "term-account", title: [Account], body: [What really happened to an occurrence, as `occurrence.account` states it: undecided, done with its final output, release key and effect, turned down, lapsed, taken back, or blocked.]),
  (slug: "term-superseding-window", title: [Superseding window], body: [The interval in which a recommendation from an older trigger could commit after `happened` ran for a newer one. It is closed for every key that was expected first.]),
  (slug: "term-stop", title: [Stop], body: [What a guard, a release check or an executor answers when it does not allow a release: `Refuse(reason)` for good, or `Unsure(reason)` when it could not tell.]),
  (slug: "term-expectation", title: [Expectation], body: [The record, made by `expect` or `expect_until` in the transaction that receives a trigger, that a recommendation is expected under a key about a subject, with the occurrence's own deadline when it has one. A newer event supersedes it before it begins.]),
  (slug: "term-not-begun", title: [Record not begun], body: [A record that holds an expectation or only failed attempts and no recommendation yet. Its head status is `pending`.]),
  (slug: "term-subject-lock", title: [Subject lock], body: [The id `happened` loads through the port, purely as a lock, before it reads which records about a subject wait: a reserved prefix and a SHA-256 of the point, the subject's kind and its id. No entry is ever stored under it.]),
  (slug: "term-hold", title: [Hold], body: [A declared wait between acceptance and release during which the decider may withdraw the output. The guard is checked again when the hold ends.]),
  (slug: "term-withdrawal", title: [Withdrawal], body: [The decider or a withdrawer taking a held output back before its hold ends by the store clock. A withdrawn occurrence is settled and nothing is released.]),
  (slug: "term-validity", title: [Validity], body: [A declared duration after which an undecided occurrence can no longer be decided, counted from the recommendation. Its end is one bound on the deadline.]),
  (slug: "term-deadline", title: [Deadline], body: [The time at or after which an occurrence can no longer be decided: its validity's end, or an instant the application gave the occurrence itself on its expectation or its proposal, whichever is earliest. At or after it, no verdict, showing, note, expansion, answer, or policy step is accepted.]),
  (slug: "term-effective-deadline", title: [Effective deadline], body: [The deadline written on the recommendation entry: the earliest of the point's validity counted from the recommendation, the deadline the record's expectations carry, and the proposal's. A recommendation never moves it later.]),
  (slug: "term-lapsed", title: [Lapsed occurrence], body: [An undecided occurrence, or a record not begun, whose deadline has passed by the store clock, whether or not the wake-up that expires it was delivered.]),
  (slug: "term-wake-up", title: [Wake-up], body: [A delivery of `wake` for an occurrence at a time the record says something is due: the end of a hold, the deadline, or a step. A wake-up that finds nothing due writes nothing.]),
  (slug: "term-superseding", title: [Superseding], body: [Ending every record of a point about a subject that has released nothing (not begun, undecided or held) because a declared world event happened to that subject. A held occurrence superseded releases nothing.]),
  (slug: "term-consulting", title: [Consulting], body: [A policy outcome that asks another decision point first. The consulted point's answer returns as facts through `inform`, and the policy current at that moment is read again with them.]),
  (slug: "term-consultation", title: [Consultation], body: [What a consult handler receives: the consulted point's name, the consulting occurrence, its subject, its context, and its recommendation.]),
  (slug: "term-sample", title: [Sample], body: [Which proceeding occurrences are reviewed after release: never, or about a stated number in a hundred, chosen by the SHA-256 hash of the occurrence id.]),
  (slug: "term-review", title: [Review], body: [A verdict given after release on a sampled occurrence. It changes no outcome and counts toward agreement.]),
  (slug: "term-reviewer", title: [Reviewer], body: [A validator who may review a sampled occurrence: by default anyone except whoever decided, asked for, recommended as their own act, or already reviewed it; with `reviewed_by`, only holders of the named set.]),
  (slug: "term-assessment", title: [Assessment], body: [What proved true later about an occurrence: right, or wrong with the output that would have been right when it is known, with its evidence, its source, and when it was known. It never overwrites a verdict or an output.]),
  (slug: "term-drift", title: [Drift], body: [An input of an occurrence, a context reference or a read, whose current revision differs from the revision the model had. A showing records each drifted input with its newer revision; drift cannot tell a change in the world from one the release itself caused.]),
  (slug: "term-card-document", title: [Card document], body: [The card as a JSON document of format `decision-card.v1`, which a client with none of the point's code can show and answer.]),
  (slug: "term-reader", title: [Reader], body: [A point's function that reads a context and its sources into a value of the point's own type. The same reader serves the model, the card, the export, and replay.]),
  (slug: "term-tally", title: [Tally], body: [Agreement and accuracy for one recommender value: how many judged occurrences agreed, how many were judged, how many were rejected outright, how many assessments were right, and how many were assessed.]),
  (slug: "term-agreement", title: [Agreement], body: [The share of judged occurrences in which the output people let through is the output the recommender proposed.]),
  (slug: "term-accuracy", title: [Accuracy], body: [The share of assessments that found the final output right.]),
  (slug: "term-example", title: [Example], body: [An occurrence as it was known at a stated time with every input resolved: the prompt, the context content, the reads, and what validators opened. An example needs nothing else to be replayed.]),
  (slug: "term-replay", title: [Replay], body: [Running a candidate model over examples, with the record as its only source, and tallying its outputs against what validators let through and what proved true.]),
  (slug: "term-follow-on", title: [Follow-on], body: [What an application starts after an occurrence settles, such as a fresh run that tells the customer. It builds its context from the world as it is, never from the waiting run's view.]),
)
